WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.280 --> 00:00:03.719
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute Papers
with Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:03.719 --> 00:00:09.370
In a recent video, we showcased a computer
graphics technique that simulated the process

00:00:09.370 --> 00:00:15.349
of baking, and now, it’s time to discuss
a paper that is about simulating how we can

00:00:15.349 --> 00:00:17.880
tear this loaf of bread apart.

00:00:17.880 --> 00:00:23.320
This paper aligns well with the favorite pastimes
of a computer graphics researcher, which is,

00:00:23.320 --> 00:00:27.760
of course, destroying virtual objects in a
spectacular fashion.

00:00:27.760 --> 00:00:32.780
Like the previous work, this new paper also
builds on top of the Material Point Method,

00:00:32.780 --> 00:00:38.350
a hybrid simulation technique that uses both
particles and grids to create these beautiful

00:00:38.350 --> 00:00:44.050
animations, however, it traditionally does
not support simulating cracking and tearing

00:00:44.050 --> 00:00:45.050
phenomena.

00:00:45.050 --> 00:00:52.019
Now, have a look at this new work, and marvel
at how beautifully this phenomenon is simulated.

00:00:52.019 --> 00:00:58.929
With this, we can smash oreos, candy crabs,
pumpkins, and much, much more.

00:00:58.929 --> 00:01:02.199
This jelly fracture scene is my absolute favorite.

00:01:02.199 --> 00:01:08.060
Now, when an artist works with these simulations,
the issue of artistic control often comes

00:01:08.060 --> 00:01:09.060
up.

00:01:09.060 --> 00:01:13.939
After all, this method is meant to compute
these phenomena by simulating physics, and

00:01:13.939 --> 00:01:18.810
we can’t just instruct physics to be more
beautiful…or can we?

00:01:18.810 --> 00:01:24.490
Well, this technique offers us plenty of parameters
to tune the simulation to our liking, two

00:01:24.490 --> 00:01:30.930
that we’ll note today are alpha, which means
the hardening, and beta is the cohesion parameter.

00:01:30.930 --> 00:01:32.990
So what does that mean exactly?

00:01:32.990 --> 00:01:38.729
Well, beta was cohesion, which is the force
that holds matter together, so as we go to

00:01:38.729 --> 00:01:44.850
the right, the objects stay more intact, and
as we go down, the objects shatter into more

00:01:44.850 --> 00:01:46.200
and more pieces.

00:01:46.200 --> 00:01:50.939
The method offers us more parameters than
these, but even with these two, we can really

00:01:50.939 --> 00:01:53.550
make the kind of simulation we are looking
for.

00:01:53.550 --> 00:01:56.520
Ah, what the heck, let’s do two more.

00:01:56.520 --> 00:02:01.420
We can even control the way the cracks form
with the Mc parameter, which is the speed

00:02:01.420 --> 00:02:11.980
of crack propagation, and G is the energy
release which, as we look to the right, increases

00:02:11.980 --> 00:02:14.790
the object’s resistance to damage.

00:02:14.790 --> 00:02:16.910
So how long does this take?

00:02:16.910 --> 00:02:22.599
Well, the technique takes its sweet time,
the execution timings range from 17 seconds

00:02:22.599 --> 00:02:24.570
to about 10 minutes per frame.

00:02:24.570 --> 00:02:29.650
This is one of those methods that does something
that wasn’t possible before, and it is about

00:02:29.650 --> 00:02:31.410
doing things correctly.

00:02:31.410 --> 00:02:36.790
And after a paper appears on something that
makes the impossible possible, followup research

00:02:36.790 --> 00:02:41.019
works get published later that further refine
and optimize it.

00:02:41.019 --> 00:02:45.880
So, as we say, two more papers down the line,
this will run much faster.

00:02:45.880 --> 00:02:50.510
Now, a word about the first author of the
paper, Joshuah Wolper.

00:02:50.510 --> 00:02:55.819
Strictly speaking, it is his third paper,
but only the second within computer graphics,

00:02:55.819 --> 00:02:59.370
and my goodness, did he come back with guns
blazing.

00:02:59.370 --> 00:03:03.819
This paper was accepted to the SIGGRAPH conference,
which is one of the biggest honors a computer

00:03:03.819 --> 00:03:09.620
graphics researcher can get, perhaps equivalent
to the olympic gold medal for an athlete.

00:03:09.620 --> 00:03:12.349
It definitely is worthy of a gold medal.

00:03:12.349 --> 00:03:16.610
Make sure to have a look at the paper in the
video description, it is an absolutely beautifully

00:03:16.610 --> 00:03:18.310
crafted piece of work.

00:03:18.310 --> 00:03:20.129
Congratulations Joshuah!

